            {
              "id": "0a09fc8e-f89f-3fe9-a456-a633a84847cc",
              "name": "Open Redirect Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Open Redirect Vulnerability due to Untrusted User Input in redirect()\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nOpen Redirect Vulnerability due to Untrusted User Input in `redirect()` is a common security flaw in JavaScript applications, particularly those using Express.js. This vulnerability occurs when an application accepts untrusted input that could cause the web application to redirect the request to a URL contained within untrusted input. By modifying untrusted URL input to a malicious site, an attacker may successfully launch a phishing scam and steal user credentials.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using user-supplied input in the `redirect()` function. If it's necessary to redirect based on user input, ensure that the supplied value is valid, safe, and intended. This can be achieved by maintaining a list of authorized or safe URLs to which you can redirect and validating user input against this list.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar app = express();\n\nvar whitelist = ['http://example1.com', 'http://example2.com']; // list of safe URLs\n\napp.get('/redirect', function(req, res) {\n    var targetURL = req.query.url;\n    if (whitelist.indexOf(targetURL) != -1) {\n        res.redirect(targetURL);\n    } else {\n        res.redirect('/'); // default redirection\n    }\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, we maintain a whitelist of URLs and only redirect to the URL if it's in the whitelist.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- Express.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-601",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0a48d04d-64cc-38fe-9333-23629fe15ade",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In HTML String May Result In XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it directly uses user input (`req.body.login`) in a SQL query without any sanitization or escaping. This allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts or SQL commands.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Use a safe API which avoids the use of the interpreter entirely or provides a parameterized interface.\n- For any data that is included in dynamic content, ensure it is properly escaped for the interpreter context it will be included in.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side.\n- Use an appropriate web application firewall.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code should be fixed to use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can prevent SQL injection attacks. Here is an example of how to do this with a hypothetical database library:\n\n```javascript\nvar sql = \"SELECT name,id FROM Users WHERE login= ?\";\nvar params = [req.body.login];\ndb.query(sql, params, function(err, results) {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the exact library dependencies. However, it seems to be using some form of SQL database library and a web framework that provides the `req.body` object.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\n## Description\n\nA potential Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability from user-controlled data in HTML string is a security flaw that occurs when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is vulnerable to XSS because it directly uses user input (`req.body.login`) in a SQL query without any sanitization or parameterization. This can lead to SQL Injection attacks, where an attacker can manipulate the SQL query to gain unauthorized access to data or perform other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. **Escape Untrusted HTTP Request Data**: Depending on the interpreter, escape all untrusted data based on the HTML context (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) that the data will be placed into.\n\n2. **Use Secure APIs**: Use APIs, such as parameterized queries, that automatically escape user input.\n\n3. **Positive or \"whitelist\" input validation**: Only accept known good input that meets a certain criteria.\n\n4. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP makes it possible for server administrators to reduce or eliminate the vectors by which XSS can occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou should use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL Injection. Here is a fixed version of your code:\n\n```javascript\nvar sql = \"SELECT name,id FROM Users WHERE login= ?\";\nvar params = [req.body.login];\nconnection.query(sql, params, function(error, results, fields) {\n  if (error) throw error;\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to be using a MySQL library for Node.js. The exact library cannot be determined from the provided code, but it could be one of the following:\n\n- `mysql`\n- `mysql2`\n- `sequelize`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0c9dac94-3a02-3481-a866-2d1de101719c",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information or allow for other security vulnerabilities to be exploited.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.User.find()` is not being properly handled. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a problem connecting to the database), the rejection will not be caught and handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have a `.catch()` block for every `.then()` block in your promises. The `.catch()` block will catch any errors that occur in the promise chain and allow you to handle them appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'login': req.body.login\n\t}\n}).then(user => {\n\tif (user) {\n\t\t// Send reset link via email happens here\n\t\treq.flash('info', 'Check email for reset link')\n\t\tres.redirect('/login')\n\t} else {\n\t\treq.flash('danger', \"Invalid login username\")\n\t\tres.redirect('/forgotpw')\n\t}\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// Handle the error here\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, a `.catch()` block has been added to handle any errors that occur when trying to find the user in the database.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A database library (such as Sequelize) to provide the `db.User.find()` function.\n- An Express.js-like library to provide the `req` and `res` objects.\n- A flash messaging library (such as connect-flash) to provide the `req.flash()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0ed5fd0d-2c74-3c4c-b277-189b95162db1",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have protection against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. In a CSRF attack, an attacker tricks a victim into performing actions on their behalf on a web application in which the victim is authenticated.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the POST route '/modifyproduct' lacks CSRF protection. This means that if an authenticated user is tricked into clicking a malicious link or loading a malicious site, the attacker could potentially modify product details on behalf of the user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement CSRF tokens in your application. CSRF tokens are unique, random values associated with a user's session, which are required to be sent with every state-changing request. This ensures that only the legitimate user can perform actions in their session, as only they have access to their CSRF token.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `csurf` middleware in Express.js to implement CSRF protection. Here's how you can modify the provided code to include CSRF protection:\n\n```javascript\nvar csurf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csurf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/modifyproduct', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.modifyProductSubmit);\n```\n\nIn this code, `csurf` middleware is added to the '/modifyproduct' POST route. This middleware generates a CSRF token and associates it with the user's session. It also validates the token sent with every request to this route.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- csurf: CSRF protection middleware for Express.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "158c36a9-9607-3953-94bc-53ddffe1b780",
              "name": "Admin Route Lacks Authorization Check",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Missing Authorization Check in Admin Route\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Missing Authorization Check in Admin Route\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application does not properly protect sensitive functionality. This can allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to privileged functions. In the context of JavaScript and Node.js applications, this often occurs when middleware used for authorization checks is not properly implemented or is missing entirely.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the route `/admin/usersapi` is protected by an authentication middleware `authHandler.isAuthenticated`. However, it does not include an authorization check to ensure that the authenticated user has the necessary permissions to access this route.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement an authorization check in your routes, especially those that provide access to sensitive functionality. This check should verify that the authenticated user has the necessary permissions to perform the requested action.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you might implement an authorization check for the `/admin/usersapi` route:\n\n```javascript\nconst isAdmin = (req, res, next) => {\n  if (req.user && req.user.role === 'admin') {\n    next();\n  } else {\n    res.status(403).send('Unauthorized');\n  }\n};\n\nrouter.get('/admin/usersapi', authHandler.isAuthenticated, isAdmin, appHandler.listUsersAPI);\n```\n\nIn this example, the `isAdmin` middleware checks if the authenticated user's role is 'admin'. If it is, the request is allowed to proceed. If not, a 403 Unauthorized response is sent.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using Express.js, a popular web application framework for Node.js. The `authHandler.isAuthenticated` and `appHandler.listUsersAPI` middlewares are not part of Express.js and are likely custom middleware functions. Therefore, the only library dependency for this code to execute properly is:\n\n- express\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "194ddd46-e637-3aac-9bd6-f279545ed3bf",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `db.sequelize.query()` is not properly handled in case of a rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include error handling for promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` method to the promise, which will be invoked when the promise is rejected. The `.catch()` method should contain logic to handle the error appropriately, such as logging the error and returning a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.sequelize.query(query, {\n\tmodel: db.User\n}).then(user => {\n\tif (user.length) {\n\t\tvar output = {\n\t\t\tuser: {\n\t\t\t\tname: user[0].name,\n\t\t\t\tid: user[0].id\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tres.render('app/usersearch', {\n\t\t\toutput: output\n\t\t})\n\t} else {\n\t\treq.flash('warning', 'User not found')\n\t\tres.render('app/usersearch', {\n\t\t\toutput: null\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}).catch(err => {\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred while processing your request.');\n});\n```\n\nIn the above code, if the promise returned by `db.sequelize.query()` is rejected, the `.catch()` method will be invoked. The error will be logged to the console and a response with a status code of 500 (Internal Server Error) will be sent to the client.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "19d2a2dc-74e6-3047-98f6-987c4094ba29",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.findAll()` is not being handled properly. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a problem connecting to the database), there is no `.catch()` block to handle the error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always include error handling for promises. This can be done by chaining a `.catch()` block to the promise, which will be executed if the promise is rejected. The `.catch()` block should handle the error in a way that does not disrupt the application or expose sensitive information.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll({\n\twhere: {\n\t\tname: {\n\t\t\t[Op.like]: '%' + req.body.name + '%'\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(products => {\n\toutput = {\n\t\tproducts: products,\n\t\tsearchTerm: req.body.name\n\t}\n\tres.render('app/products', {\n\t\toutput: output\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// Handle the error here. For example, you might log the error and render an error page.\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).render('error', { error: err });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.Product.findAll()`)\n- Express (for `req` and `res`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1b1b1c48-c47c-359a-9cdc-094d5ac6f7db",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the query is returning all fields of the product with the specified id, which may include fields that should not be exposed to the user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to be returned.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.find({\n    where: {\n        'id': req.query.id\n    },\n    attributes: ['id', 'name', 'description'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(product => {\n    // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `find` method will only return the `id`, `name`, and `description` fields of the product.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1fae96e7-4e84-34c7-bfea-ce5a023516f1",
              "name": "Insecure Logging Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unprotected SQL Logging in Sequelize Initialization\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnprotected SQL Logging in Sequelize Initialization is a vulnerability that occurs when the Sequelize initialization does not properly sanitize or escape SQL queries before logging them. This can lead to sensitive information being exposed in the logs, such as database credentials, table structures, or even the data itself. This vulnerability can be exploited by an attacker to gain unauthorized access to the database or perform other malicious activities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable SQL logging in production environments. If logging is necessary, ensure that sensitive information is properly sanitized or redacted before being logged. Additionally, you should use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\nvar sequelize = new Sequelize(process.env.DATABASE_URL, {\n  logging: false\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, the `logging` option is set to `false` to disable SQL logging.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: This is an Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) library for Node.js. It provides a high-level API for interacting with SQL databases.\n- `dotenv`: This is a zero-dependency module that loads environment variables from a `.env` file into `process.env`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
