            {
              "id": "20337bae-297b-3366-92c6-67b903a9206e",
              "name": "Dynamic Value Used With Urllib May Lead To File Read",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUntrusted URL handling can permit local file disclosure or server-side request forgery. Python's urllib.request.urlopen accepts multiple URL schemes, including file://, so allowing an attacker-controlled value to reach this sink can expose files readable by the application process.\n\nValidate the URL before creating the request and permit only the schemes and destinations required by the application. The fix below accepts HTTPS requests only, restricts hosts to an explicit allowlist, and blocks redirects so a permitted URL cannot redirect the client to an unapproved destination.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass request parameters, headers, database values, or other untrusted data directly to urlopen. Prefer constructing URLs from trusted configuration and fixed path templates. If callers must supply a URL, parse it and enforce an exact scheme, a small hostname allowlist, an expected port, and any required path prefix before making the request. Apply equivalent validation to every redirect, or disable redirects when they are not needed. Do not allow file, data, ftp, or other non-HTTP(S) schemes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit\nfrom urllib.request import HTTPRedirectHandler, Request, build_opener\n\nALLOWED_HOSTS = {\"api.example.com\"}\n\n\nclass NoRedirectHandler(HTTPRedirectHandler):\n    def redirect_request(self, request, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):\n        return None\n\n\ndef fetch_api_response(url: str) -> bytes:\n    parsed = urlsplit(url)\n    hostname = parsed.hostname\n\n    if (\n        parsed.scheme != \"https\"\n        or not hostname\n        or hostname.lower() not in ALLOWED_HOSTS\n        or parsed.username is not None\n        or parsed.password is not None\n    ):\n        raise ValueError(\"URL must use HTTPS and target an approved host\")\n\n    request = Request(url, method=\"GET\")\n    opener = build_opener(NoRedirectHandler)\n    with opener.open(request, timeout=30) as response:\n        return response.read()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nNo additional packages; Python standard library modules urllib.parse and urllib.request.\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Server-Side Request Forgery](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n- [CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html)\n- [CWE-22: Path Traversal](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)\n- [Python urllib.request documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/urllib.request.html)\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n`vulnerable_fetch.py`\n\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport urllib.request\n\n\ndef fetch_url(url: str) -> bytes:\n    # Vulnerable: attacker-controlled URL reaches urlopen without validation.\n    request = urllib.request.Request(url, method=\"GET\")\n\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=30) as response:\n        return response.read()\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Fetch a URL and print its contents.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"url\",\n        help=\"URL to fetch, for example: file:///tmp/demo.txt\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    body = fetch_url(args.url)\n    print(body.decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\"))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n`pyproject.toml`\n\n```toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=68\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"vulnerable-url-fetcher\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = []\n\n[project.scripts]\nvulnerable-fetch = \"vulnerable_fetch:main\"\n```\n\nReproduce locally without accessing sensitive files:\n\n```bash\nprintf 'local demonstration content\\n' > /tmp/demo.txt\npython vulnerable_fetch.py file:///tmp/demo.txt\n```\n\nThe unvalidated command-line URL can use `file://` and other schemes supported by `urllib.request.urlopen`, allowing local file reads or SSRF when attacker-controlled input reaches `fetch_url`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-918",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2210a9d6-f3a3-3d11-9eaa-757cf47b15cd",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the Promise returned by `db.User.findAll({})` is not being handled for a case where it might get rejected.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections by providing a `.catch()` block or by using `try/catch` with async/await. This allows you to handle any errors that occur during the execution of the Promise and prevent unexpected application behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findAll({})\n  .then(users => {\n    res.status(200).json({\n      success: true,\n      users: users\n    });\n  })\n  .catch(err => {\n    console.error(err);\n    res.status(500).json({\n      success: false,\n      message: 'An error occurred while fetching users'\n    });\n  });\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, if the Promise returned by `db.User.findAll({})` is rejected, the `.catch()` block will handle the error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using Sequelize (for `db.User.findAll({})`) and Express (for `res.status().json()`). So, the library dependencies required by the code example to execute properly are:\n\n- Sequelize\n- Express\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP - Error Handling](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Error_Handling_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
