            {
              "id": "24e62fbb-cc51-3bda-9264-8b50b8e25682",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unverified User Input IDOR Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nInsecure Direct Object References (IDOR) vulnerability occurs when an application provides direct access to objects based on user-supplied input. In this case, the application uses user input directly to query its data without any additional validation or authorization checks. This can allow an attacker to bypass authorization and directly access data.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is using the user-supplied input (`req.body.id`) directly to query the database for a user. This can lead to IDOR vulnerability if the `id` is predictable or guessable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Ensure that the user-supplied input is valid and expected. For example, you can check if the `id` is a number and within the expected range.\n\n2. Implement proper access controls: Ensure that the user is authorized to access the requested data. For example, you can check if the user is the owner of the requested user data.\n\n3. Use indirect object references: Instead of using direct references to database records, use indirect references that are mapped to the user's session.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\n// Validate user input\nif (!Number.isInteger(req.body.id)) {\n  throw new Error('Invalid input');\n}\n\n// Check user authorization\nif (req.user.id !== req.body.id) {\n  throw new Error('Unauthorized');\n}\n\n// Query the database\ndb.User.find({\n  where: {\n    'id': req.body.id\n  }\n}).then(user => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js, used to handle HTTP requests and responses.\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
