            {
              "id": "25522f94-f42e-30ca-be00-6ae69db87632",
              "name": "Assigning To InnerHTML Can Introduce DOM XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Potential DOM XSS Vulnerability from Assigning User-Controlled Data to innerHTML\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nA potential DOM XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is assigned to `innerHTML` in JavaScript. This is because `innerHTML` interprets any HTML that it is given, including `<script>` tags, which can be used to execute malicious scripts. In the provided code snippet, `users[i].id` is assigned to `innerHTML`, which could be a potential security risk if `users[i].id` contains user-controlled data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `innerHTML` to directly insert user-controlled data into your HTML. Instead, consider using `textContent` or `innerText`, which do not interpret the assigned values as HTML. Alternatively, you can sanitize the user-controlled data before assigning it to `innerHTML`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nc_id.innerHTML = users[i].id;\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```javascript\nc_id.textContent = users[i].id;\n```\n\nOr, if you need to insert HTML, sanitize the user-controlled data first:\n\n```javascript\nc_id.innerHTML = sanitize(users[i].id);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to have any library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2e4a519a-8118-31d1-aace-e3fb22ddc603",
              "name": "Assigning To InnerHTML Can Introduce DOM XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Potential DOM XSS Vulnerability from Assigning User-Controlled Data to innerHTML\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nA potential DOM XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability can occur when user-controlled data is assigned to `innerHTML` in JavaScript. This is because `innerHTML` interprets any HTML that it is given, which includes potentially malicious scripts. If an attacker can control the input that is assigned to `innerHTML`, they can inject a script that will be executed by the browser.\n\nIn the provided code example, the user's email is directly assigned to `innerHTML`. If the email is not properly sanitized and contains a script, it will be executed when the email is displayed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `innerHTML` with user-controlled data. Instead, use `textContent` or `innerText`, which do not interpret HTML and therefore prevent script execution. \n\nIf you must use `innerHTML`, ensure that the user-controlled data is properly sanitized to remove any potential scripts. This can be done using a library that provides HTML sanitization, such as DOMPurify.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable line of code with one of the following, depending on your needs:\n\n```javascript\n// Option 1: Use textContent\nc_email.textContent = users[i].email;\n\n// Option 2: Use innerText\nc_email.innerText = users[i].email;\n\n// Option 3: Use DOMPurify to sanitize the email before assigning it to innerHTML\nc_email.innerHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(users[i].email);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies to execute properly. However, if you choose to use DOMPurify for sanitization, you will need to include it in your project.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS (Cross Site Scripting) Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
