            {
              "id": "304c736c-8339-3edd-8a47-866f035a5f6c",
              "name": "Predictable Token Using Weak Hashing",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "MD5 User-Controlled Input Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nMD5 User-Controlled Input Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a security flaw where an attacker can manipulate the input to the MD5 hash function, potentially leading to unauthorized access or other security breaches. In the provided code snippet, the application is comparing a user-provided token (req.query.token) with the MD5 hash of another user-provided input (req.query.login). If an attacker can predict or control the 'login' input, they can potentially generate a matching 'token' and bypass the security check.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid using MD5 for security-sensitive operations**: MD5 is a weak hash function with known vulnerabilities. For security-sensitive operations like password storage or token generation, use a stronger hash function like SHA-256 or bcrypt.\n\n2. **Avoid directly comparing user-controlled inputs for authentication**: Instead of directly comparing user-provided inputs for authentication, use a secure method like password-based key derivation functions (PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt).\n\n3. **Use a secure method for token generation**: Instead of generating tokens based on user-provided input, use a secure, random method for token generation. This reduces the risk of an attacker being able to predict or control the token.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\n\n// Generate a secure, random token\nfunction generateToken() {\n    return crypto.randomBytes(64).toString('hex');\n}\n\n// Store the token securely\nlet token = generateToken();\nlet hashedToken = bcrypt.hashSync(token, 10);\n\n// When checking the token, use bcrypt's compare function\nif (bcrypt.compareSync(req.query.token, hashedToken)) {\n    // The tokens match\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- bcrypt\n- crypto\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP - Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-328: Reversible One-Way Hash](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-330",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
