            {
              "id": "36ab68b6-b208-391a-a5a6-c2dfbf99a3a3",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.find()` is not handled properly. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a database error), there is no `.catch()` block to handle the error, which can lead to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises. This allows you to define how your application should behave in the event of a rejection, such as logging the error or returning a user-friendly error message.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is the corrected code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t}\n}).then(product => {\n\tif (!product) {\n\t\tproduct = new db.Product()\n\t}\n\tproduct.code = req.body.code\n\tproduct.name = req.body.name\n\tproduct.description = req.body.description\n\tproduct.tags = req.body.tags\n\tproduct.save().then(p => {\n\t\tif (p) {\n\t\t\treq.flash('success', 'Product added/modified!')\n\t\t\tres.redirect('/app/products')\n\t\t}\n\t}).catch(err => {\n\t\toutput = {\n\t\t\tproduct: product\n\t\t}\n\t\treq.flash('danger',err)\n\t\tres.render('app/modifyproduct', {\n\t\t\toutput: output\n\t\t})\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\t// Handle the error appropriately here\n});\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- A database library (such as Sequelize) to provide the `db.Product.find()` and `product.save()` methods.\n- An Express.js-like library to provide the `req` and `res` objects and methods.\n- A flash messaging library (such as connect-flash) to provide the `req.flash()` method.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
