            {
              "id": "3e9e987b-f0b1-3370-981a-cd804cd05dae",
              "name": "Dynamic Value Used With Urllib May Lead To File Read",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUntrusted URL handling can permit local-file access. Passing an attacker-controlled value to urllib.request.urlopen can allow a file:// URL to read files accessible to the application process.\n\nValidate URLs before making the request and permit only explicitly required HTTPS hosts. Replace urllib for this outbound HTTP use case with requests, which does not support file:// URLs, and disable automatic redirects unless each redirect is separately validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not construct request URLs directly from user input. Prefer selecting from server-side endpoint identifiers or mapping user-provided identifiers to fixed URLs. If dynamic URLs are required, parse them and enforce an HTTPS-only scheme, an exact allowlist of hostnames, allowed ports, and appropriate path restrictions. Disable redirects or validate every redirect target with the same policy. Run the service with least-privilege filesystem permissions so that a separate defect cannot expose sensitive local files.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit\n\nimport requests\n\nALLOWED_HOSTS = {\"api.example.com\"}\n\n\ndef fetch_api_url(url: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        parsed = urlsplit(url)\n        valid_port = parsed.port in (None, 443)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid URL\") from exc\n\n    if (\n        parsed.scheme != \"https\"\n        or parsed.hostname not in ALLOWED_HOSTS\n        or not valid_port\n        or parsed.username is not None\n        or parsed.password is not None\n    ):\n        raise ValueError(\"URL is not an approved HTTPS endpoint\")\n\n    with requests.get(url, timeout=60, allow_redirects=False) as response:\n        response.raise_for_status()\n        return response.text\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install requests\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Server-Side Request Forgery Prevention](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n- [CWE-73: External Control of File Name or Path](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/73.html)\n- [Python urllib.parse documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/urllib.parse.html)\n- [Requests Quickstart](https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/quickstart/)\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n> **Warning:** Intentionally vulnerable demonstration. Use only in an isolated test environment.\n\n```python\n# vulnerable_fetch.py\nimport argparse\nimport tempfile\nimport urllib.request\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef fetch_url(url: str) -> str:\n    # Vulnerable: attacker-controlled URLs may use file:// to read local files.\n    request = urllib.request.Request(url)\n\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=60) as response:\n        return response.read().decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Intentionally vulnerable URL fetch demonstration.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"url\",\n        nargs=\"?\",\n        help=\"URL to fetch, including file:// URLs for the demonstration.\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--demo\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Create and read a temporary local file using a file:// URL.\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    if args.demo:\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:\n            fixture = Path(directory) / \"local-secret.txt\"\n            fixture.write_text(\"DEMO_LOCAL_FILE_CONTENT\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n            url = fixture.as_uri()\n            print(f\"Fetching test fixture: {url}\")\n            print(fetch_url(url), end=\"\")\n        return\n\n    if not args.url:\n        parser.error(\"provide a URL or use --demo\")\n\n    print(fetch_url(args.url), end=\"\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"vulnerable-url-fetch-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = []\n\n[project.scripts]\nvulnerable-url-fetch = \"vulnerable_fetch:main\"\n```\n\nRun the safe local fixture demonstration:\n\n```console\npython vulnerable_fetch.py --demo\n```\n\nThe vulnerable sink accepts `file://` URLs because no scheme or host validation occurs before `urllib.request.urlopen`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-918",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3f3c6f29-d6c1-395a-87b8-75f265db3544",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes, and can potentially expose sensitive information in error messages.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `user.save()` is not handled properly. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a problem saving the user), there is no `.catch()` block to handle the error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises. This block should handle the error appropriately, which may include logging the error, retrying the operation, or informing the user that an error occurred.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nuser.save().then(function () {\n    req.flash('success', \"Password successfully reset\");\n    res.redirect('/login');\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle the error appropriately here\n    console.error(error);\n    req.flash('error', \"An error occurred while resetting the password\");\n    res.redirect('/reset-password');\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A promise-based library for interacting with a database (such as Mongoose if you're using MongoDB)\n- Express.js for handling HTTP requests and responses\n- Connect-flash for flash message middleware in Express.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "409c0470-0d6d-3030-ad5f-4e2191206657",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the `findOne` function fails for any reason (e.g., the database is down, there's a network error, etc.), the error will not be caught and handled, leading to an Unhandled Promise Rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always have error handling in place for promises. This can be done using `.catch()` blocks or by using `try/catch` with async/await. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findOne({\n    where: {\n        'id': uid\n    }\n}).then(function (user) {\n    if (user) {\n        done(null, user);\n    } else {\n        done(null, false)\n    }\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle the error here\n    console.error(error);\n    done(error);\n});\n```\n\nIn this updated code, if `findOne` fails, the error will be caught and logged to the console, and the `done` callback will be called with the error.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to be using Sequelize, a promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server. Therefore, the following libraries are required:\n\n- sequelize\n- A database driver compatible with Sequelize (e.g., pg for Postgres, mysql2 for MySQL, etc.)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
