            {
              "id": "4755beb1-3945-31bf-9b76-0f3ddfb6293b",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unverified User Input IDOR Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnverified User Input IDOR (Insecure Direct Object References) Vulnerability is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when a function with no proper access control is used to access specific resources directly by modifying the value of a parameter used in a direct object reference. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when the application uses user-supplied input to retrieve a database record without verifying if the user is authorized to access that record.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the application is using the user-supplied login name (`req.body.login`) to find a user in the database. If an attacker can guess or brute-force the login name of another user, they can potentially access or modify that user's data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement proper access control checks to ensure that the user is authorized to access the requested resource. You should also validate and sanitize all user inputs to prevent SQL Injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\n// Assuming `currentUser` is the logged-in user\nif (req.body.login === currentUser.login) {\n  db.User.find({\n    where: {\n      'login': req.body.login\n    }\n  }).then(user => {\n    // ...\n  });\n} else {\n  // Handle unauthorized access attempt\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we're checking if the login name in the request matches the login name of the currently logged-in user. If they don't match, we handle it as an unauthorized access attempt.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js for handling HTTP requests (`req.body.login`).\n- Sequelize.js for querying the database (`db.User.find`).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "48b8013d-d557-3332-9145-6f6042113cc3",
              "name": "Admin Route Lacks Authorization Check",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Missing Authorization Check in Admin Route\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Missing Authorization Check in Admin Route\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application does not properly protect sensitive functionality. This can allow an attacker to gain unauthorized access to privileged functions, such as administrative functions. In the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the fact that the route `/admin/users` is not properly protected by an authorization check.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always perform authorization checks on sensitive routes. This can be done by implementing middleware functions that verify the user's role before allowing them to access certain routes. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, the `authHandler.isAuthenticated` middleware function is used to authenticate users. However, authentication is not enough for sensitive routes like `/admin/users`. You should also check if the authenticated user has the necessary privileges to access this route. \n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nrouter.get('/admin/users', [authHandler.isAuthenticated, authHandler.isAdmin], function(req, res){\n    res.render('app/adminusers')\n})\n```\n\nIn this version, `authHandler.isAdmin` is a middleware function that checks if the authenticated user is an admin. This function should return a 403 status code if the user is not an admin.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the Express.js library for routing and the `authHandler` middleware for authentication. Therefore, the following libraries are required:\n\n- express\n- authHandler (this seems to be a custom library, so the actual name might be different)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-285",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-285"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4ba9ffd4-57a1-3ef8-b205-fb18267e4011",
              "name": "Command Injection Vulnerability In Shell Command",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Command Injection Vulnerability through User Input Concatenation\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nCommand Injection Vulnerability through User Input Concatenation is a serious security flaw that can occur in JavaScript applications. This vulnerability arises when an application passes unsafe user-supplied data (forms, cookies, HTTP headers, etc.) to a system shell. In this case, the attacker can execute arbitrary commands on the host operating system using a maliciously crafted input.\n\nThe provided code snippet is vulnerable to command injection because it concatenates a user-supplied value (`req.body.address`) directly into a command that is executed by the system shell.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user input directly in system commands. If it's necessary to include user input in system commands, the input should be properly sanitized and validated. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using the `child_process.execFile` function, which does not allow shell parameter expansion:\n\n```javascript\nvar execFile = require('child_process').execFile;\n\nexecFile('ping', ['-c', '2', req.body.address], function(err, stdout, stderr) {\n    output = stdout + stderr;\n    res.render('app/ping', {\n        output: output\n    });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js (for handling HTTP requests and responses)\n- child_process (Node.js built-in module for executing system commands)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e20ab92-5416-3d56-b05e-d175dadeae7f",
              "name": "Path Traversal Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Potential Path Traversal Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nA Potential Path Traversal Vulnerability, also known as Directory Traversal, is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application. This is done by manipulating variables that reference files with \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences and its variations. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user-supplied input is not properly sanitized and is used to construct a file path for file operations.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `sequelize.import()` function is used to import a file. If the `file` variable is user-controlled and not properly sanitized, it could lead to a path traversal vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid using user-supplied input to construct file paths directly.\n2. Use a whitelist of approved inputs.\n3. If user input must be used, ensure it is properly sanitized and validated. For example, disallow input containing \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences and its variations.\n4. Use built-in functions to construct file paths, such as `path.resolve()` and `path.normalize()` in Node.js.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```javascript\nvar path = require('path');\nvar file = /* user-supplied input */;\nfile = path.basename(file);\nvar model = sequelize.import(path.join(__dirname, file));\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `path.basename()` function is used to get the last part of a path. This effectively prevents any \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences from traversing directories.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Path: A core Node.js module for handling and transforming file paths.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)\n\n## Description\n\nA Potential Path Traversal Vulnerability, also known as Directory Traversal, is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application. This is done by manipulating variables that reference files with \"dot-dot-slash (../)\" sequences and its variations. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user-supplied input is used to construct a pathname that is intended to identify a file or directory located underneath a restricted parent directory.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability exists in the `sequelize.import()` function where the `file` variable is directly used to join the path. If an attacker can control the `file` variable, they can potentially read any file on the server.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate user inputs and avoid using user-supplied input directly in file path construction. If it's necessary to use user-supplied input, ensure that it's properly sanitized to prevent any path traversal sequences.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```javascript\nvar path = require('path');\nvar file = req.body.file;\n\n// validate the file variable\nif (!file.match(/^[a-z0-9_.-]*$/i)) {\n    res.status(400).send('Invalid file name.');\n    return;\n}\n\n// use the sanitized file variable\nvar model = sequelize.import(path.join(__dirname, file));\n```\n\nIn this fix, we are validating the `file` variable to ensure it only contains alphanumeric characters, underscores, hyphens, and dots. This prevents any path traversal sequences from being used.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `path`: The Path module provides utilities for working with file and directory paths.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
