            {
              "id": "58198161-e516-3982-8232-399ce77639fa",
              "name": "Untrusted Input Can Lead To Command Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Command Injection Vulnerability due to Untrusted Input in Application Execution\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nCommand Injection is a type of vulnerability where an attacker is able to execute arbitrary commands on the host operating system via a vulnerable application. This vulnerability risk occurs when an application passes unsafe user-supplied data (forms, cookies, HTTP headers, etc.) to a system shell. In this case, the attacker-supplied operating system commands are usually executed with the privileges of the vulnerable application.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is vulnerable to command injection because it directly uses user input (`req.body.address`) in a system command (`ping -c 2 ' + req.body.address`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using user input directly in system commands. If it's necessary to include user input in system commands, the input should be properly sanitized and validated. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nvar address = req.body.address;\nif (validator.isIP(address)) {\n    exec('ping -c 2 ' + address, function (err, stdout, stderr) {\n        // ...\n    });\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid IP address\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, the `validator.isIP()` function from the `validator` library is used to check if the user input is a valid IP address before it's used in the system command.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `child_process`: This is a built-in Node.js module used to execute system commands.\n- `express`: This is a web application framework for Node.js used to handle HTTP requests.\n- `validator`: This is a library of string validators and sanitizers.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-77: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command ('Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/77.html)\n\n## Description\n\nCommand Injection Vulnerability due to Untrusted Input in Application Execution is a serious security flaw that occurs when an application passes unsafe user-supplied data (forms, cookies, HTTP headers, etc.) to a system shell. In this case, the application uses the user input to construct a command to be executed. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary commands on the host operating system using a vulnerable application.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is executing a shell command that includes user-supplied input (`req.body.address`). This input is not validated or sanitized, making the application vulnerable to command injection attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using shell commands in your application. If possible, use libraries or modules that provide the needed functionality without requiring a shell command.\n\n2. If you must use shell commands, always validate and sanitize user-supplied input. This can help prevent an attacker from injecting malicious commands.\n\n3. Use parameterized APIs or use proper escaping before including the user input in the command.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's a recommended fix for the provided code using the `child_process.execFile()` function, which does not allow shell parameter substitution:\n\n```javascript\nvar execFile = require('child_process').execFile;\n\nexecFile('ping', ['-c', '2', req.body.address], function(err, stdout, stderr) {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `child_process`: This is a built-in Node.js module and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-77: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command ('Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/77.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
