            {
              "id": "58f37fb3-5791-3a3e-9b6f-e121432dfd65",
              "name": "Predictable Token Using Weak Hashing",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "MD5 User-Controlled Input Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nMD5 User-Controlled Input Vulnerability in JavaScript refers to a security flaw where an attacker can manipulate the input to the MD5 hash function, potentially leading to unauthorized access or other security breaches. MD5 is a widely used cryptographic hash function that produces a 128-bit (16-byte) hash value. It is commonly used to verify data integrity. However, MD5 is not collision-resistant; as such, it is possible to find different inputs that hash to the same output, which is a vulnerability.\n\nIn the given code snippet, the application is comparing a token from the request body to an MD5 hash of a login from the request body. If an attacker can predict or control the `login` value, they might be able to craft a `token` that matches the MD5 hash, leading to a potential security breach.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider the following recommendations:\n\n1. **Avoid using MD5 for security-sensitive operations**: MD5 is considered broken in terms of collision resistance. For any security-sensitive operations, consider using stronger hash functions like SHA-256.\n\n2. **Use a Salt with your Hash**: A salt is a random data that is used as an additional input to a one-way function that hashes data, a password or passphrase. Salts are used to safeguard passwords in storage.\n\n3. **Use existing secure libraries for authentication**: Instead of implementing your own authentication mechanism, consider using existing libraries that have been thoroughly tested and vetted by the community.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the bcrypt library for hashing and checking the password:\n\n```javascript\nconst bcrypt = require('bcrypt');\n\n// Hash the password\nconst salt = bcrypt.genSaltSync(10);\nconst hashedPassword = bcrypt.hashSync(req.body.login, salt);\n\n// Store hashedPassword in the database\n\n// Check the password\nif (bcrypt.compareSync(req.body.token, hashedPassword)) {\n  // Passwords match\n} else {\n  // Passwords don't match\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- bcrypt: A library to help you hash passwords.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-328: Reversible One-Way Hash](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-330",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-330"
                ]
              }
