            {
              "id": "5a50b736-5b73-3cc7-8c1c-f70e99cbec40",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `findAll({})` function is used without specifying the fields to be returned, which means all fields from the `User` table will be returned, including potentially sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `findAll` function that specifies the fields to be returned.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findAll({\n    attributes: ['id', 'username', 'email'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(users => {\n    res.status(200).json({\n        success: true,\n        users: users\n    })\n})\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, only the `id`, `username`, and `email` fields will be returned from the `User` table.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- Express: A fast, unopinionated, and flexible Node.js web application framework.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "63477eba-0399-3c04-92f0-e6025397cb27",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnselected Field Database Query Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a developer does not explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data if the query is used to return results directly to the client. An attacker can potentially gain access to data that they are not authorized to view.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly specify the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `findAll` method that specifies the fields to be returned.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll({\n  attributes: ['id', 'name', 'description'] // specify the fields to be returned\n}).then(products => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `findAll` method is passed an object that specifies that only the 'id', 'name', and 'description' fields should be returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "649ef06b-8197-3b90-b556-386f21d792c4",
              "name": "Insecure Deserialization Can Lead To Remote Code Execution",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Detected Insecure Deserialization Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nInsecure Deserialization is a vulnerability that occurs when untrusted data is used to abuse the logic of an application, inflict a denial of service (DoS) attack, or execute arbitrary code upon it being deserialized. It also happens to be one of the OWASP Top 10 Application Security Risks.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is deserializing data from an untrusted source (`req.files.products.data.toString('utf8')`) without validating or sanitizing it first. This can lead to various security issues, including remote code execution, privilege escalation, and other serious data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid deserialization of untrusted data, if possible.\n2. Implement input validation. Do not rely on client-side validation only, as it can be easily bypassed.\n3. Use digital signatures to verify the source of serialized objects.\n4. Limit the classes of objects that can be deserialized.\n5. Log deserialization exceptions and failures, as they can indicate an attack.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, you can mitigate the vulnerability by validating the input before deserializing it. Here's an example of how you can do this:\n\n```javascript\nvar productsData = req.files.products.data.toString('utf8');\n\nif (isValid(productsData)) {\n    var products = serialize.unserialize(productsData);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid data\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `isValid` is a function that you would implement to validate the input data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `serialize` library for serialization and deserialization of data.\n- An HTTP library (like `express`) to handle the request (`req`).\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 - A8:2017-Insecure Deserialization](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A8_2017-Insecure_Deserialization)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-502",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65030232-f99c-3c71-9697-ef22772470db",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the database query fails for any reason (e.g., the database is down, there's a network error, etc.), the Promise rejection will not be handled, and the application may crash or behave unexpectedly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle Promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to the Promise chain, which will catch any errors that occur in the preceding `.then()` blocks. In the `.catch()` block, you should handle the error appropriately, such as by logging the error and returning a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you could modify the provided code to handle Promise rejections:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'login': req.query.login\n\t}\n}).then(user => {\n\tif (user) {\n\t\tif (req.query.token == md5(req.query.login)) {\n\t\t\tres.render('resetpw', {\n\t\t\t\tlogin: req.query.login,\n\t\t\t\ttoken: req.query.token\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\treq.flash('danger', \"Invalid reset token\")\n\t\t\tres.redirect('/forgotpw')\n\t\t}\n\t} else {\n\t\treq.flash('danger', \"Invalid login username\")\n\t\tres.redirect('/forgotpw')\n\t}\n}).catch(err => {\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code appears to require the following libraries:\n\n- A database library (such as Sequelize) for `db.User.find()`\n- Express.js for `req`, `res`, and `req.query`\n- A flash messages library (such as connect-flash) for `req.flash()`\n- The md5 library for `md5()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "69f8443d-6b76-3506-af0b-8b5795d5c1fe",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, crashes, and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the Promise returned by `db.User.findOne()` is not being caught if it gets rejected, which could lead to this vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always include a `.catch()` block when working with Promises to handle any potential errors. This allows you to control the application flow even when an error occurs, and prevents the application from crashing or behaving unexpectedly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the provided code with a `.catch()` block added to handle any potential errors:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findOne({\n    where: {\n        'login': username\n    }\n}).then(function (user) {\n    if (!user) {\n        return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n    }\n    if (!isValidPassword(user, password)) {\n        return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n    }\n    return done(null, user);\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle the error here\n    console.error(error);\n    return done(error);\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.findOne()`)\n- Express (for `req.flash()`)\n- Passport (for `done()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6afaab93-ad55-39a5-a7d4-66581a06b018",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to query the `User` database with a `login` field from the request body. If the `User` database contains sensitive fields, they could be returned in the query result and potentially exposed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly define the fields to be returned from a database query. This can be done using the `attributes` option in the `find` method. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n    attributes: ['login'], // Only return the 'login' field\n    where: {\n        'login': req.body.login\n    }\n}).then(user => {\n    // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `attributes` option is used to explicitly define that only the `login` field should be returned from the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: Fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "740c22eb-3230-38b4-830b-9c13894f68db",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but the rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, if the `user.save()` promise is rejected, there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the rejection.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises. The `.catch()` block will be executed when the promise is rejected, allowing you to handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n    where: {\n        'login': req.body.login\n    }\n}).then(user => {\n    if (user) {\n        if (req.body.token == md5(req.body.login)) {\n            user.password = bCrypt.hashSync(req.body.password, bCrypt.genSaltSync(10), null)\n            user.save().then(function () {\n                req.flash('success', \"Password successfully reset\")\n                res.redirect('/login')\n            }).catch(err => {\n                console.error(err);\n                req.flash('danger', \"An error occurred while resetting password\")\n                res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n            })\n        } else {\n            req.flash('danger', \"Invalid reset token\")\n            res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n        }\n    } else {\n        req.flash('danger', \"Invalid login username\")\n        res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n    }\n}).catch(err => {\n    console.error(err);\n    req.flash('danger', \"An error occurred while finding user\")\n    res.redirect('/forgotpw')\n})\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.find()`)\n- Express.js (for `req`, `res`, `req.body`, `req.flash()`, `res.redirect()`)\n- bcryptjs (for `bCrypt.hashSync()`, `bCrypt.genSaltSync()`)\n- md5 (for `md5()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
