            {
              "id": "7c0c7640-a2d5-3af7-9adc-9263a3e90fd8",
              "name": "Hardcoded Credentials In Source Code",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Detected Hard-Coded Credential Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nDetected Hard-Coded Credential Vulnerability refers to the insecure practice of embedding plaintext credentials directly within the source code. This vulnerability can lead to unauthorized access, data leakage, and potential system compromise if the source code is exposed or accessible. In JavaScript, this vulnerability is often found when developers hard-code sensitive information like API keys, database credentials, or in this case, a secret key for session management.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hard-coding credentials in your source code. Instead, use environment variables, configuration files, or secure vaults to store sensitive information. These methods allow you to separate your credentials from your source code, reducing the risk of exposure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hard-coding the secret directly in your code, you can use an environment variable to store it. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\nconst secret = process.env.SECRET || 'default_secret';\n```\n\nIn this example, the secret is retrieved from the environment variable `SECRET`. If the environment variable is not set, it defaults to `'default_secret'`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not provide enough context to determine the required library dependencies. However, if the secret is used for session management, it's likely that the `express-session` library is required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Hardcoded Secrets](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html#hardcoded-secrets)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7c425869-4a5b-39e2-b2c3-678fb09c1208",
              "name": "Unescaped EJS Output Allows XSS Attacks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Detected Unescaped EJS Output Vulnerability Allowing XSS Attacks\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Detected Unescaped EJS Output Vulnerability Allowing XSS Attacks\" is a vulnerability in JavaScript programming language that occurs when user input is directly embedded into the webpage without proper sanitization or escaping. This allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts into the webpage, which are then executed by the browser. This type of attack is known as Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).\n\nEJS (Embedded JavaScript) is a templating engine for JavaScript. The `<%- include(vuln_description) %>` syntax is used to include a file and directly output its contents into the template. If `vuln_description` contains user input and is not properly sanitized, it can lead to XSS attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always sanitize user input before including it in the webpage. This can be done by using the `<%= %>` syntax instead of `<%- %>`, which automatically escapes HTML characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `<%- include(vuln_description) %>` with `<%= include(vuln_description) %>`. This will ensure that any HTML characters in `vuln_description` are properly escaped.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- EJS\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7e8fb4c0-39b6-3c06-8ea5-e32e5a9fff36",
              "name": "Unescaped EJS Output Allows XSS Attacks",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "EJS Output Unescaped Vulnerability Detected: Potential for XSS Attacks\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"EJS Output Unescaped Vulnerability Detected: Potential for XSS Attacks\" is a vulnerability in JavaScript programming language that occurs when user input is included in the output without proper sanitization or validation. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive information like session cookies, or perform other harmful actions.\n\nEJS (Embedded JavaScript) is a templating engine used in JavaScript. The `<%- include(vuln_scenario) %>` syntax is used to include raw output in the template. If `vuln_scenario` contains user input, this can lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user input that is included in the output. This can be done using the `<%= include(vuln_scenario) %>` syntax in EJS, which automatically escapes the output.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the vulnerable code:\n\n```javascript\n<%- include(vuln_scenario) %>\n```\n\nwith the fixed code:\n\n```javascript\n<%= include(vuln_scenario) %>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `ejs` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7eb7a67d-89f2-37e3-b65c-e4fa3be37804",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"Unselected Field Database Query Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that occurs when a developer does not explicitly define the fields to be returned in a database query. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data. In the provided code, the `find` method is used to query the `User` database, but no specific fields are selected, so all fields associated with the user will be returned, including potentially sensitive ones.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should always explicitly define the fields to be returned in a database query. This can be done by passing an object to the `find` method that specifies the fields to include or exclude. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t},\n\tattributes: ['id', 'username', 'email'] // specify the fields to return\n}).then(user => {\n\t// ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `attributes` option is used to specify the fields to be returned by the query. Only the `id`, `username`, and `email` fields will be returned.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-209: Information Exposure Through an Error Message](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7edc0b19-6cd6-3a9d-a61b-f2e6b4ccd34e",
              "name": "Untrusted Input Can Lead To SQL Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "SQL Injection Risk from Untrusted Input and Raw SQL Query Concatenation\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks. This is due to the fact that it directly concatenates user input (`req.body.login`) into a SQL query string. An attacker could potentially manipulate the `login` parameter to alter the SQL query, leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this risk, it is recommended to use parameterized queries or prepared statements, which can effectively separate SQL logic from data, thereby preventing an attacker from manipulating the SQL query.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using parameterized queries:\n\n```javascript\nvar query = \"SELECT name,id FROM Users WHERE login= :login\";\ndb.sequelize.query(query, {\n\tmodel: db.User,\n\treplacements: { login: req.body.login }\n}).then(user => {\n\t// rest of the code\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// error handling\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- Express.js (for handling requests and responses)\n- Sequelize (for interacting with the database)\n- Connect-flash (for flash messages)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-89",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8b7015f8-770c-358e-bba0-24263e0a559c",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In XML Parsers Can Lead To XXE",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"XXE Processing Vulnerabilities in XML Parsers with User-Controlled Data\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nXXE (XML External Entity) Processing Vulnerabilities in XML Parsers with User-Controlled Data is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when an application processing XML input allows the injection of external entities by a user. This can lead to the disclosure of internal files, denial of service, server side request forgery, port scanning from the perspective of the machine where the parser is located, and other system impacts.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is using the `libxmljs` library to parse XML data from a user-controlled source (`req.files.products.data.toString('utf8')`). The options `{noent:true,noblanks:true}` allow the parsing of external entities (`noent:true`), which opens up the possibility for an XXE attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable the parsing of external entities in the XML parser configuration. If the parsing of external entities is not required for your application, this is the most effective way to prevent XXE attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nvar products = libxmljs.parseXmlString(req.files.products.data.toString('utf8'), {noent:false,noblanks:true})\n```\n\nIn this fix, the `noent` option is set to `false`, which disables the parsing of external entities.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `libxmljs`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference ('XXE')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-611",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8d0dc7b0-9014-3517-ac7c-42708d488023",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as SQL Injection, is a common security vulnerability that allows an attacker to manipulate SQL queries. In JavaScript, this vulnerability can occur when user input is directly used in a SQL query without proper sanitization or parameterization. In the provided code, the user input `req.body.name` is directly concatenated into a SQL `LIKE` clause, which could allow an attacker to manipulate the query.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, user input should never be directly used in a SQL query. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements. These techniques ensure that user input is always treated as literal data, not part of the SQL command.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code using Sequelize's built-in parameterization:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.findAll({\n\twhere: {\n\t\tname: {\n\t\t\t[Op.like]: Sequelize.literal(`%${req.body.name}%`)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(products => {\n\t// ...\n});\n```\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following libraries:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: Fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "931995f3-23d7-34e1-b773-a7c4eaf32155",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have protection against Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. In a CSRF attack, an attacker tricks a victim into performing actions on their behalf on a web application in which the victim is authenticated. \n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the POST route '/bulkproducts' does not have any CSRF protection. This means that if an authenticated user is tricked into clicking a malicious link or visiting a malicious website, the attacker can make POST requests to '/bulkproducts' on behalf of the user.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should implement CSRF protection on your POST routes. This can be done by using CSRF tokens. A CSRF token is a random, unique value associated with a user's session, which must be included in all form submissions. When a form is submitted, the server checks the CSRF token to ensure it matches the one stored in the user's session.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can use the `csurf` middleware in Express.js to add CSRF protection to your routes. Here is how you can modify your code:\n\n```javascript\nvar csrf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/bulkproducts', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.bulkProducts);\n```\n\nIn this code, `csrfProtection` is a middleware function that validates the CSRF token in the request. If the token is missing or invalid, it sends a 403 Forbidden response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- `csurf`: CSRF protection middleware for Express.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "933c0859-c78e-3c4b-ac8e-7ea875fcbbbc",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejections Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but there is no error handling mechanism in place to catch and handle the error. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including crashes and potential security vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the promise returned by `db.User.findOne` and `db.User.create` are not handled properly. If these promises are rejected (for example, due to a database error), the rejection will not be caught and handled, leading to an unhandled promise rejection.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by attaching a `.catch()` block to the promise, which will be called if the promise is rejected. The `.catch()` block should handle the error appropriately, for example by logging the error and returning a response to the client indicating that an error occurred.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is the provided code with added error handling:\n\n```javascript\ndb.User.findOne({\n    where: {\n        'email': username\n    }\n}).then(function (user) {\n    if (user) {\n        return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Account Already Exists'));\n    } else {\n        if (req.body.email && req.body.password && req.body.username && req.body.cpassword && req.body.name) {\n            if (req.body.cpassword == req.body.password) {\n                db.User.create({\n                    email: req.body.email,\n                    password: createHash(password),\n                    name: req.body.name,\n                    login: username\n                }).then(function (user) {\n                    return done(null, user)\n                }).catch(function (error) {\n                    // Handle error\n                    console.error(error);\n                    return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'An error occurred'));\n                });\n            } else {\n                return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Passwords dont match'));\n            }\n        } else {\n            return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Input field(s) missing'));\n        }\n    }\n}).catch(function (error) {\n    // Handle error\n    console.error(error);\n    return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'An error occurred'));\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.User.findOne` and `db.User.create`)\n- Express (for `req` and `res`)\n- Connect-flash (for `req.flash`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_security_cheat_sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
