            {
              "id": "96bf9b6a-fcf1-3eab-be8e-47ba3cb8bd10",
              "name": "Insecure Logging Configuration",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unprotected SQL Logging in Sequelize Initialization\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"Unprotected SQL Logging in Sequelize Initialization\" vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when the Sequelize ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) library is initialized without properly configuring the logging option. By default, Sequelize logs all SQL queries it executes. If these logs are not properly protected, they can expose sensitive information such as passwords, credit card numbers, or other personally identifiable information (PII). This can lead to serious security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should disable Sequelize's logging feature in production environments. If logging is necessary for debugging or other purposes, ensure that the logs are properly protected and that sensitive information is either not logged or is obfuscated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can disable logging by setting the `logging` option to `false` when initializing Sequelize:\n\n```javascript\nvar sequelize = new Sequelize(config.database, config.username, config.password, {\n    host: config.host,\n    dialect: config.dialect,\n    logging: false\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: This is the Sequelize ORM library.\n- `config`: This is not a library, but a configuration object that should contain the database connection details.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-532: Information Exposure Through Log Files](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-532",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-532"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9b0a2c63-a010-3479-a131-e071cb4a9bee",
              "name": "Directly Writing To Response Object May Cause XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Direct Writing to Response Object May Lead to XSS Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a web application through an untrusted source, most frequently a web request, and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability is present when the application does not adequately validate, filter, or encode user-supplied input before including it in the generated HTML content.\n\nIn the context of JavaScript and Node.js, directly writing to the response object (e.g., `res.send(html)`) without properly sanitizing the `html` content can lead to XSS vulnerabilities. This is because an attacker can inject malicious scripts into the `html` content, which will then be executed by the browser when the HTML content is rendered.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize and escape user-supplied input before including it in the generated HTML content. This can be done using various libraries such as `DOMPurify` or `xss`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix using the `xss` library:\n\n```javascript\nvar xss = require('xss');\n\n// ...\n\nvar sanitizedHtml = xss(html);\nres.send(sanitizedHtml);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `xss` library. You can install it using npm:\n\n```\nnpm install xss\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9d8c3327-ae28-32f7-877d-4c72615629b9",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unverified User Input IDOR Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnverified User Input IDOR (Insecure Direct Object References) Vulnerability is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when a function with no access control is used to process user input. This can allow an attacker to bypass authorization and directly access resources in the system. In the given JavaScript code, the vulnerability exists because the `login` parameter in the `find` function is directly using user input from `req.body.login` without any validation or sanitization.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust user input blindly.\n2. Implement proper access control mechanisms to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive data.\n3. Use parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL Injection attacks.\n4. Regularly update and patch your systems and libraries to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the given code:\n\n```javascript\nconst Sequelize = require('sequelize');\nconst Op = Sequelize.Op;\n\ndb.User.find({\n  where: {\n    'login': {\n      [Op.eq]: req.body.login\n    }\n  }\n}).then(user => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this fix, we are using Sequelize's `Op.eq` operator to ensure that the `login` value is exactly equal to `req.body.login`. This prevents the possibility of SQL Injection attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite, and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9d9c1240-eb9b-35fe-9ed3-d8eed633fa39",
              "name": "Insecure Cookie Settings",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Unspecified Domain in Default Session Middleware Settings Vulnerability\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"Unspecified Domain in Default Session Middleware Settings Vulnerability\" in JavaScript is a security flaw that can occur when the domain attribute in the session cookie is not specified. This can lead to potential session hijacking or cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks as the session cookie can be sent to all subdomains of the parent domain, some of which may be malicious or vulnerable to attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to explicitly set the domain attribute in the session cookie settings. This restricts the session cookie to be sent only to the specified domain, reducing the risk of session hijacking or XSS attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nvar express = require('express');\nvar session = require('express-session');\nvar cookieParser = require('cookie-parser');\n\nvar app = express();\napp.use(cookieParser());\n\napp.use(session({\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { \n    secure: false,\n    domain: 'yourdomain.com' // specify your domain here\n  }\n}));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- express\n- express-session\n- cookie-parser\n\nYou can install these dependencies using npm:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express express-session cookie-parser\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-346: Origin Validation Error](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-522",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/522.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-522"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a6ca5928-3a02-30c6-abff-24477424338e",
              "name": "Insecure File Permissions",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nOverly permissive file permissions allow any local user to read, modify, or execute the generated output. In Python, os.chmod(output, 0o777) grants read, write, and execute access to the owner, group, and all other users.\n\nUse the least restrictive mode required by the output file. For a regular output file that only its owner must modify, 0o644 permits owner read/write access while allowing others to read it without granting write or execute access.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAssign permissions based on the sensitivity and intended consumers of the file. Use 0o600 for private files containing secrets or user data, 0o640 when a specific group requires read access, and 0o644 only for non-sensitive files intended to be readable by local users. Do not grant execute permission to regular data files. Ensure the parent directory is also restricted appropriately, and create sensitive files with restrictive permissions from the outset to avoid a temporary exposure window.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport os\n\nos.chmod(output, 0o644)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPython standard library: os\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Web Security Testing Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/latest/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/01-Information_Gathering/05-Review_Webpage_Comments_and_Metadata)\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)\n- [Python os.chmod documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.chmod)\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n`app.py`\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport argparse\nimport os\nimport stat\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef create_output(output: Path) -> None:\n    output.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    with output.open(\"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n        file.write(\"Generated output\\n\")\n\n    # Vulnerable: grants read, write, and execute permissions to everyone.\n    os.chmod(output, 0o777)\n\n    mode = stat.S_IMODE(output.stat().st_mode)\n    print(f\"Created: {output}\")\n    print(f\"Permissions: {mode:04o}\")\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Create an output file with insecure permissions.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--output\",\n        default=\"generated-output.txt\",\n        help=\"Path of the generated file (default: generated-output.txt)\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    create_output(Path(args.output))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n`pyproject.toml`\n\n```toml\n[build-system]\nrequires = []\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[project]\nname = \"insecure-chmod-example\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible example of an overly permissive os.chmod call\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```console\npython app.py --output ./output/generated-output.txt\nls -l ./output/generated-output.txt\n```\n\nExpected permissions include world-readable, world-writable, and world-executable mode:\n\n```console\n-rwxrwxrwx ... generated-output.txt\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-276",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/276.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-276"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ae5be0ea-85a4-3b69-8e33-cdbd99d871ce",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\" in JavaScript occurs when a Promise is rejected, but this rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior, including potential crashes or unhandled exceptions. In the context of Node.js, an unhandled promise rejection can cause the Node.js process to terminate.\n\nIn the provided code, the promise returned by `db.Product.find()` is not being handled for potential rejection. If the promise is rejected (for example, if there is a problem connecting to the database), the rejection will not be caught and the application may crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises, which will catch any errors that occur during the execution of the promise.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\ndb.Product.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'id': req.query.id\n\t}\n}).then(product => {\n\tif (!product) {\n\t\tproduct = {}\n\t}\n\toutput = {\n\t\tproduct: product\n\t}\n\tres.render('app/modifyproduct', {\n\t\toutput: output\n\t})\n}).catch(err => {\n\t// Handle the error here\n\tconsole.error(err);\n\tres.status(500).send('An error occurred');\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, if the promise is rejected, the `.catch()` block will be executed, logging the error and sending a 500 response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Sequelize (for `db.Product.find()`)\n- Express (for `req.query.id` and `res.render()`)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Unhandled Promise Rejection](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#unhandled-promise-rejections)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-755",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/755.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-755"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b1654fa1-d62e-325d-ace5-658ec6a29678",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In HTML String May Result In XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. In this case, the user-controlled data (`req.body.address`) is directly used in an `exec` function, which can lead to command injection if the user input is not properly sanitized. Moreover, the output of the command is directly rendered in the HTML without any sanitization, which can lead to XSS attacks if the command output contains any HTML tags.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using `exec` with user-controlled data. If you must use it, make sure to sanitize the user input properly.\n2. Always sanitize any data that is going to be rendered in the HTML. This can be done using various libraries that provide HTML encoding functions.\n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is a possible fix using the `child_process` library's `execFile` function, which does not allow command injection, and the `escape-html` library to sanitize the output:\n\n```javascript\nvar escape = require('escape-html');\nvar execFile = require('child_process').execFile;\n\nexecFile('ping', ['-c', '2', req.body.address], function (err, stdout, stderr) {\n    output = escape(stdout + stderr);\n    res.render('app/ping', {\n        output: output\n    });\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `express`: for handling HTTP requests and responses.\n- `child_process`: for executing shell commands.\n- `escape-html`: for sanitizing the output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Command Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n\n## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability in the provided code snippet is that user-controlled data (`req.body.address`) is directly used in the `exec` function, which can lead to arbitrary command execution. Additionally, the output of the command execution is directly rendered in the HTML response without proper output encoding, which can lead to potential XSS attacks if the command execution output contains malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Avoid using user-controlled data in command execution. If it's necessary, validate and sanitize the user input strictly.\n2. Use output encoding libraries to encode the output of the command execution before rendering it in the HTML response.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nconst sanitize = require('validator').escape;\nconst exec = require('child_process').exec;\n\nlet address = sanitize(req.body.address);\nexec('ping -c 2 ' + address, function (err, stdout, stderr) {\n    let output = sanitize(stdout + stderr);\n    res.render('app/ping', {\n        output: output\n    });\n});\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, the `validator` library is used to sanitize the user-controlled data before using it in the `exec` function and before rendering it in the HTML response.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- Express.js: A web application framework for Node.js.\n- Validator: A library of string validators and sanitizers.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
