            {
              "id": "bd13d313-bbc8-3d82-a845-9458e026db7a",
              "name": "Dynamic Value Used With Urllib May Lead To File Read",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUntrusted URL handling can permit local file disclosure and server-side request forgery. In Python, urllib.request.urlopen() supports multiple URL schemes, including file://, so allowing an attacker-controlled value to reach this sink can expose files readable by the application process.\n\nThe call shown opens the supplied request directly. Restrict URLs to an explicit HTTPS scheme and a defined set of expected destination hosts before making the request. Use an HTTP-specific client so non-HTTP schemes such as file:// are not accepted, and do not follow redirects unless redirected destinations are independently validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass user-controlled URLs directly to urllib.request.urlopen. Prefer constructing destination URLs from trusted configuration and validated path or query components. If callers must provide a URL, parse it and enforce an exact scheme, hostname allowlist, permitted port policy, and, where applicable, a restricted path pattern. Reject file://, data:, ftp:, and all other non-HTTP schemes. Disable redirects or validate every redirect target with the same policy. For stronger SSRF protection, ensure approved hosts cannot resolve to loopback, link-local, private, or cloud metadata IP ranges, and enforce network egress controls outside the application.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit\n\nimport requests\n\nALLOWED_HOSTS = {\"api.example.com\"}\n\n\ndef fetch_remote_resource(url: str) -> bytes:\n    parsed = urlsplit(url)\n    if parsed.scheme != \"https\" or parsed.hostname not in ALLOWED_HOSTS:\n        raise ValueError(\"URL must use HTTPS and target an approved host\")\n    if parsed.username is not None or parsed.password is not None:\n        raise ValueError(\"URLs containing credentials are not allowed\")\n\n    response = requests.get(\n        url,\n        timeout=(5, 120),\n        allow_redirects=False,\n    )\n    response.raise_for_status()\n    return response.content\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install requests\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Server-Side Request Forgery](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Server_Side_Request_Forgery)\n- [CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html)\n- [Python urllib.request documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/urllib.request.html)\n- [Requests Quickstart](https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/quickstart/)\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n`app.py`\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nEducational SSRF/local-file-disclosure demonstration.\n\nThe optional URL argument is attacker-controlled and reaches urlopen() without\nscheme or host validation. Run only in an isolated local environment.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport pathlib\nimport tempfile\nimport urllib.request\n\n\ndef fetch(url: str) -> bytes:\n    request = urllib.request.Request(url)\n\n    # Vulnerable sink: accepts file:// and other urllib-supported schemes.\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=120) as response:\n        return response.read()\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Demonstrate unsafe urllib.request.urlopen URL handling.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"url\",\n        nargs=\"?\",\n        help=\"Attacker-controlled URL, e.g. file:///path/to/file\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    demo_file: pathlib.Path | None = None\n    if args.url:\n        target_url = args.url\n    else:\n        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(\n            mode=\"w\",\n            prefix=\"urllib-demo-\",\n            suffix=\".txt\",\n            delete=False,\n            encoding=\"utf-8\",\n        ) as handle:\n            handle.write(\"Demo local secret: this file was read through file://\\n\")\n            demo_file = pathlib.Path(handle.name)\n\n        target_url = demo_file.as_uri()\n        print(f\"No URL supplied; using safe demo URL: {target_url}\")\n\n    try:\n        content = fetch(target_url)\n        print(\"--- Response body ---\")\n        print(content.decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\"))\n    finally:\n        if demo_file is not None:\n            demo_file.unlink(missing_ok=True)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n`requirements.txt`\n\n```text\n# No third-party dependencies; uses Python standard library only.\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\npython app.py\n```\n\nOr demonstrate the vulnerable attacker-controlled input path:\n\n```bash\npython app.py 'file:///tmp/example.txt'\n```"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-918",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c04545fa-e5e3-37be-8c6f-6c464b21fae9",
              "name": "Insecure Direct Object Reference Without Access Control",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unverified User Input IDOR Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nInsecure Direct Object References (IDOR) vulnerability occurs when an application provides direct access to objects based on user-supplied input. In this case, the application uses user-supplied input without proper validation or authorization checks. This can allow an attacker to manipulate these references to access unauthorized data.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the application is directly using the user-supplied input (`req.body.id`) to query the database for a product. If an attacker manipulates the `id` parameter in the request, they could potentially access data they are not authorized to view.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, the application should perform proper input validation and authorization checks before using user-supplied input to access data. \n\n## Code Fix\n\nHere is a simple example of how you could validate and authorize the user input before using it to query the database:\n\n```javascript\nif (req.user && req.user.id === req.body.id) {\n\tdb.Product.find({\n\t\twhere: {\n\t\t\t'id': req.body.id\n\t\t}\n\t}).then(product => {\n\t\t// ...\n\t});\n} else {\n\t// Handle unauthorized access attempt...\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the application checks if the user is authenticated and if the authenticated user's ID matches the ID provided in the request. If not, the application rejects the request.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example seems to be using the following libraries:\n\n- Express.js (for handling HTTP requests)\n- Sequelize (for querying the database)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A5-Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A5_2017-Broken_Access_Control)\n- [CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-639",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c183bb47-9c87-3d38-bf40-65eaba0f7c7b",
              "name": "Failed Login Attempt Without Explicit Logging",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unlogged Failed Authentication Attempt Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"Unlogged Failed Authentication Attempt Vulnerability\" in JavaScript refers to the situation where failed login attempts are not logged by the system. This can be a security risk as it allows attackers to attempt to gain unauthorized access to the system without being detected. In the provided code, the system does not log failed login attempts, which means that an attacker could repeatedly try to log in with different credentials without raising any alarms.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to implement logging for failed login attempts. This can help to identify and block potential brute force attacks. Additionally, consider implementing account lockouts or delays after a certain number of failed login attempts to further deter attackers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to add logging to the provided code:\n\n```javascript\npassport.use('login', new LocalStrategy({\n        passReqToCallback: true\n    },\n    function (req, username, password, done) {\n        db.User.findOne({\n            where: {\n                'login': username\n            }\n        }).then(function (user) {\n            if (!user) {\n                console.log('Failed login attempt for username: ' + username); // Log failed login attempt\n                return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n            }\n            if (!isValidPassword(user, password)) {\n                console.log('Failed login attempt for username: ' + username); // Log failed login attempt\n                return done(null, false, req.flash('danger', 'Invalid Credentials'))\n            }\n            return done(null, user);\n        });\n    }));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `passport`\n- `passport-local`\n- `express-flash`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-287: Improper Authentication](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-778",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/778.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-778"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c19db147-b975-32c4-8756-28106ee3991d",
              "name": "Missing CSRF Protection For State-Changing Forms",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe \"POST Route Lacks CSRF Protection Vulnerability\" is a security flaw in a web application where the POST route does not have Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) protection. CSRF is an attack that tricks the victim into submitting a malicious request. It uses the identity and privileges of the victim to perform an undesired function on their behalf.\n\nIn the given JavaScript code, the POST route '/useredit' lacks CSRF protection. This means that if an attacker can trick a user into clicking a link or loading a page, the attacker can make requests to this route with the user's cookies, potentially leading to unauthorized actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use CSRF tokens. A CSRF token is a unique, random value associated with a user's session. It is used to verify that the user is the one who initiated a request. \n\nIn Express.js, you can use the `csurf` middleware to add CSRF protection to your routes. \n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nFirst, install the `csurf` middleware:\n\n```bash\nnpm install csurf\n```\n\nThen, use it in your application:\n\n```javascript\nvar csrf = require('csurf');\nvar csrfProtection = csrf({ cookie: true });\n\nrouter.post('/useredit', csrfProtection, authHandler.isAuthenticated, appHandler.userEditSubmit);\n```\n\nThis will add CSRF protection to the '/useredit' route. The `csurf` middleware will add a `_csrf` property to the `req.body` object. You should include this value as a hidden field in your forms.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- express\n- csurf\n- Any libraries required by `authHandler` and `appHandler`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP CSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-352",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-352"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c6f882aa-7e66-30be-810b-64a4fa9750ea",
              "name": "Database Query Without Field Selection Exposes Data",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unfiltered Database Query Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnfiltered Database Query Vulnerability, also known as NoSQL Injection, is a type of security vulnerability that occurs when an application doesn't properly validate or escape input before using it in a database query. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the query, potentially leading to unauthorized data access, data corruption, or even data loss.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the fact that the `login` parameter from the request query is directly used in the database query without any validation or sanitization. This means that an attacker could potentially manipulate the `login` parameter to alter the database query.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it in a database query. This can be done using various methods, such as using prepared statements, parameterized queries, or ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) libraries that automatically handle these issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you could fix the provided code using Sequelize, an ORM library for Node.js:\n\n```javascript\nconst { Op } = require('sequelize');\n\ndb.User.find({\n\twhere: {\n\t\t'login': {\n\t\t\t[Op.eq]: req.query.login\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}).then(user => {\n\t// ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this code, we're using Sequelize's `Op.eq` operator to ensure that the `login` parameter is used as a direct equality comparison in the database query, preventing any potential manipulation of the query.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be using the Sequelize ORM library for Node.js. Therefore, the `sequelize` package would be required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Query Parameterization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Query_Parameterization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP: Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet in Java](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet_in_Java.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c8556768-5efa-38c0-b36c-8966a146923a",
              "name": "Child Process Module Imported Without Input Validation",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Validate user input before using in shell commands with imported child_process module.\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe `child_process` module in JavaScript provides the ability to spawn child processes in a manner that is similar, but not identical, to popen(3). This can be particularly dangerous if you're executing shell commands that include unvalidated user input. This can lead to command injection vulnerabilities where an attacker can execute arbitrary commands on the host system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it in shell commands. This can be done by using input validation libraries or by implementing your own validation logic. \n\nAvoid using the `exec` function with user input. Instead, use `execFile` or `spawn` which do not spawn a shell by default and are therefore less vulnerable to command injection.\n\n## Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```javascript\nconst exec = require('child_process').exec;\nlet user_input = req.body.user_input;\nexec('ls ' + user_input, (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nYou should use:\n\n```javascript\nconst { execFile } = require('child_process');\nlet user_input = req.body.user_input;\nexecFile('ls', [user_input], (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the built-in `child_process` module in Node.js. No additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n\n## Description\n\nIn JavaScript, the `child_process` module provides the ability to spawn child processes in a manner that is similar, but not identical, to popen(3). This can be particularly dangerous if you're executing shell commands that include unvalidated user input. This can lead to command injection vulnerabilities where an attacker can inject arbitrary commands to be executed on the server.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input before using it in shell commands. This includes:\n\n- Using input validation techniques such as whitelisting or input validation libraries to ensure only expected input is processed.\n- Avoiding the use of shell commands where possible and use safer alternatives like `child_process.execFile()`.\n- If shell commands must be used, ensure user input is properly escaped or quoted to prevent command injection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's an example of how you can validate and sanitize user input before using it in a shell command:\n\n```javascript\nconst exec = require('child_process').exec;\nconst sanitize = require('some-input-sanitization-library');\n\nlet userInput = getUserInputSomehow();\n\n// Sanitize the user input\nlet sanitizedInput = sanitize(userInput);\n\nexec(`someCommand ${sanitizedInput}`, (error, stdout, stderr) => {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nIn this example, `sanitize` is a hypothetical function that you would replace with whatever input sanitization library or function you are using.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the built-in `child_process` module in Node.js. If you're using an input sanitization library as recommended, you would also need that library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
