              "id": "dc91c059-b8d7-3ca2-81bb-4e6950613c8c",
              "name": "SQL Injection Due To Unsanitized User Input",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Potential SQL Injection Detected in Sequelize Statement due to Unsanitized User Input\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"Potential SQL Injection Detected in Sequelize Statement due to Unsanitized User Input\" is a security vulnerability that occurs when an application uses unsanitized user input in a Sequelize query. This can allow an attacker to manipulate the SQL query and perform unauthorized actions on the database, such as viewing, modifying, or deleting data. This is a type of SQL Injection attack, which is a common and serious security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always sanitize user input before using it in a SQL query. This can be done by using parameterized queries or prepared statements, which ensure that user input is always treated as data and not as part of the SQL command.\n\nIn addition, you should also validate user input on the server side to ensure it meets the expected format and does not contain any malicious characters or sequences. This can be done using regular expressions or a validation library.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to use a parameterized query with Sequelize to prevent SQL Injection:\n\n```javascript\nlet userId = req.params.id; // user input\n\ndb.sequelize.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = :userId', \n  { \n    replacements: { userId: userId }, \n    type: db.sequelize.QueryTypes.SELECT \n  })\n  .then(users => {\n    // ...\n  });\n```\n\nIn this example, `:userId` is a placeholder that Sequelize will replace with the sanitized `userId` value.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n- `express`: A fast, unopinionated, minimalist web framework for Node.js.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-89: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command ('SQL Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
