            {
              "id": "de1f49a6-2920-3aef-8b5c-f910e778fc83",
              "name": "Explicit Unescape In EJS Template",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "EJS Template XSS Vulnerability Detected Due to Unescaped External Data\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\n\"EJS Template XSS Vulnerability Detected Due to Unescaped External Data\" is a vulnerability that occurs when user-supplied data is not properly sanitized or escaped before being rendered in an EJS template. This can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, where an attacker can inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can steal sensitive information, perform actions on behalf of the user, or exploit other vulnerabilities in the user's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always escape user-supplied data before rendering it in an EJS template. This can be done using the `<%- %>` syntax in EJS, which automatically escapes HTML characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `searchTerm` is not being escaped before being rendered. This can be fixed by changing the `<%- %>` syntax to `<%= %>`, which automatically escapes HTML characters.\n\n```javascript\nListing products with <strong>search query: </strong> <%= output.searchTerm %>\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- EJS\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dfc2ca0a-57df-3a9a-a0cc-e7b038fbf747",
              "name": "Insecure Cookie Settings Without HttpOnly Flag",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unset `httpOnly` in Default Session Middleware Settings Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe `httpOnly` flag is a security feature of cookies. When this flag is set, the cookie cannot be accessed through client-side scripts. This helps to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks, as the cookie cannot be stolen via JavaScript.\n\nIn the provided code, the `httpOnly` flag is not set in the session middleware settings. This means that the session cookie can be accessed through client-side scripts, which could potentially lead to an XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always set the `httpOnly` flag to `true` for session cookies. This will prevent the cookie from being accessed through client-side scripts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the provided code:\n\n```javascript\napp.use(session({\n  secret: 'keyboard cat',\n  resave: true,\n  saveUninitialized: true,\n  cookie: { secure: false, httpOnly: true }\n}))\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the `httpOnly` flag is set to `true` for the session cookie.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `express-session`\n\nYou can install it using npm:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express-session\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - HttpOnly](https://owasp.org/www-community/HttpOnly)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-522",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/522.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-522"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e03af7d2-f1de-3621-833f-d754941defe6",
              "name": "User Controlled Data In HTML String May Result In XSS",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided code, the application is taking user input directly through `req.params.vuln` and including it in the HTML response without any sanitization or encoding. This could allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into the HTML content, leading to a potential XSS attack.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Never insert untrusted data except in allowed locations.\n- HTML escape before inserting untrusted data into HTML element content.\n- Attribute escape before inserting untrusted data into HTML common attributes.\n- JavaScript escape before inserting untrusted data into JavaScript data values.\n- Always validate user input for length, characters, format, and business rules before accepting the data to be displayed or stored.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nThe Express.js framework provides a way to escape HTML through the use of template engines like EJS or Pug. Here is an example of how you can escape HTML using EJS:\n\n```javascript\nres.render('vulnerabilities/layout', {\n\tvuln: req.params.vuln,\n\tvuln_title: vulnDict[req.params.vuln],\n\tvuln_scenario: req.params.vuln + '/scenario',\n\tvuln_description: req.params.vuln + '/description',\n\tvuln_reference: req.params.vuln + '/reference',\n\tvulnerabilities:vulnDict\n}, function (err, html) {\n\tif (err) {\n\t\tconsole.log(err)\n\t\tres.status(404).send('404')\n\t} else {\n\t\tres.send(html)\n\t}\n})\n```\n\nIn your EJS template, you would escape the output like this:\n\n```ejs\n<%= vuln %>\n```\n\nThe `<%=` tags will HTML escape the output.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following dependencies:\n\n- Express.js\n- EJS or another template engine\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)\n\n## Description\n\nThe code snippet provided is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. XSS is a type of injection, in which malicious scripts are injected into otherwise benign and trusted websites. In this case, the vulnerability arises from the fact that user-controlled data is directly used in the HTML string without proper sanitization or encoding. This allows an attacker to inject malicious scripts, which will be executed in the context of the victim's browser.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always escape user-controlled data before using it in HTML strings. This can be done using various methods, such as HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, CSS encoding, and URL encoding, depending on the context in which the data is used.\n\nIn addition, you should also consider using Content Security Policy (CSP) to reduce the risk of XSS attacks. CSP provides a standard method for website owners to declare approved sources of content that browsers should be allowed to load on their website.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you should escape the user-controlled data before using it in the HTML string. Here is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```javascript\nres.render('vulnerabilities/layout', {\n\tvuln: escape(req.params.vuln),\n\tvuln_title: escape(vulnDict[req.params.vuln]),\n\tvuln_scenario: escape(req.params.vuln + '/scenario'),\n\tvuln_description: escape(req.params.vuln + '/description'),\n\tvuln_reference: escape(req.params.vuln + '/reference'),\n\tvulnerabilities: vulnDict\n}, function (err, html) {\n\tif (err) {\n\t\tconsole.log(err)\n\t\tres.status(404).send('404')\n\t} else {\n\t\tres.send(html)\n\t}\n})\n```\n\nPlease note that the `escape` function used in the code above is a placeholder. You should replace it with a proper escaping function provided by your web application framework or a trusted library.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet provided seems to be using the Express.js framework and its rendering engine. Therefore, the following libraries are required:\n\n- express\n- any compatible template engine (e.g., pug, ejs, etc.)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Content Security Policy Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
