            {
              "id": "e9af2c2f-796a-31f5-b7d1-e8128751ed05",
              "name": "Deprecated Mathjs Eval Vulnerable To Code Injection",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "\"Deprecated mathjs.eval() is susceptible to code injection; use mathjs.evaluate() instead.\"\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nThe `mathjs.eval()` function in the Math.js library is deprecated due to its susceptibility to code injection attacks. This function evaluates a string as a mathematical expression within the JavaScript environment, which can lead to arbitrary code execution if a malicious user can control the input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should avoid using the `mathjs.eval()` function and instead use the `mathjs.evaluate()` function, which is safer and not susceptible to code injection attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the deprecated `mathjs.eval()` function with the `mathjs.evaluate()` function. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```javascript\n// Vulnerable code\noutput: mathjs.eval(req.body.eqn)\n\n// Fixed code\noutput: mathjs.evaluate(req.body.eqn)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
