            {
              "id": "f78cb2b8-1941-36cb-ada9-23487f1c57e7",
              "name": "Promise Without Catch Handler",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "Unhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability\n\n## Standalone Example (not your code)\n\n_A self-contained program written to illustrate this weakness, not taken from your project. Depending on the finding it may reproduce the vulnerable pattern, show the fix, or both; apply the Recommended Code Fix above to your code._\n\n## Description\n\nUnhandled Promise Rejection Vulnerability in JavaScript occurs when a promise is rejected, but the rejection is not caught or handled. This can lead to unexpected application behavior and potential security vulnerabilities. In the context of Sequelize, this could mean a failure to connect to the database, but the error is not properly handled, potentially leading to application crashes or unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always handle promise rejections. This can be done by adding a `.catch()` block to your promises, which will catch any errors that occur during the execution of the promise. This allows you to handle the error gracefully, such as by logging the error and returning a default value.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```javascript\nconst Sequelize = require('sequelize');\nconst sequelize = new Sequelize('database', 'username', 'password');\n\nsequelize\n  .authenticate()\n  .then(() => {\n    console.log('Connection has been established successfully.');\n  })\n  .catch((err) => {\n    console.error('Unable to connect to the database:', err);\n  });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `sequelize`: A promise-based Node.js ORM for Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, SQLite and Microsoft SQL Server.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n- [CWE-754: Improper Check for Unusual or Exceptional Conditions](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
